1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,492
Hand someone an ordinary coffee mug

2
00:00:02,492 --> 00:00:03,703
for five minutes,

3
00:00:03,703 --> 00:00:05,839
and they instantly value it at

4
00:00:05,839 --> 00:00:08,759
roughly double what an identical stranger

5
00:00:08,759 --> 00:00:09,400
will pay.

6
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,208
In a classic 1990 Journal of

7
00:00:11,208 --> 00:00:13,791
Political Economy experiment at Cornell,

8
00:00:13,791 --> 00:00:14,824
Daniel Kahneman,

9
00:00:14,824 --> 00:00:15,663
Jack Knetsch,

10
00:00:15,663 --> 00:00:17,923
and Richard Thaler found mug owners

11
00:00:17,923 --> 00:00:20,376
demanded around seven dollars to sell,

12
00:00:20,376 --> 00:00:22,830
while buyers offered near three-fifty.

13
00:00:22,830 --> 00:00:25,342
This Endowment Effect quietly clogs your

14
00:00:25,342 --> 00:00:26,534
portfolio and home:

15
00:00:26,534 --> 00:00:28,795
you cling to underperforming stocks,

16
00:00:28,795 --> 00:00:29,862
stalled projects,

17
00:00:29,862 --> 00:00:32,060
and unused gear simply because they

18
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:33,190
are already yours.

19
00:00:33,190 --> 00:00:36,994
Break ownership bias with the Cash-Equivalent Test:

20
00:00:36,994 --> 00:00:37,963
ask yourself,

21
00:00:37,963 --> 00:00:40,126
if I held this asset's market

22
00:00:40,126 --> 00:00:41,618
value in cash today,

23
00:00:41,618 --> 00:00:43,930
would I buy it again right now?

24
00:00:43,930 --> 00:00:46,008
If the honest answer is no,

25
00:00:46,008 --> 00:00:48,547
sell the dead weight and redeploy

26
00:00:48,547 --> 00:00:51,010
your capital where it compounds.
